          "heading": "1.3 Nonlinear B-H curves and hysteresis",
          "content": "## 1.3 Nonlinear B-H curves and hysteresis\n\n### Physical model and mathematical relationship\n\nA ferromagnet has B(H), not a constant mu. Use an interpolation of measured B-H points and solve NI = sum H_i l_i by iteration. Hysteresis creates loop area proportional to loss per volume per cycle. The core procedure is to select a reference polarity and sign convention, distinguish an ideal relation from an experimentally calibrated one, and account for quantities whose units differ. Electromagnetic quantities must be checked against the associated conservation law: electric current and flux linkage describe excitation; voltages and torques describe observable exchange; and losses reduce usable output. In rotating or time-varying equipment, steady-state expressions must not be extrapolated to startup without tracking stored energy.\n\n### Fully worked numerical example\n\nA simplified hysteresis model with 300 J/m^3 cycle loss in a 0.002 m^3 core at 50 Hz dissipates 30 W. It is an estimate: real loss varies with waveform, flux density, temperature and material processing. Write the given quantities in SI units before substitution, then compute the predicted response and its physical meaning. If an ideal result conflicts with a device rating, the correct engineering action is to question the simplifying assumptions rather than force the physical device to satisfy the equation. For a measured system, use its nameplate, empirical characteristic curves and operating limits to refine the estimate.\n\n### Failure modes, units, and boundary conditions\n\nDo not use the linear-reluctance estimate above saturation; increasing current then produces disproportionately small increases in flux. A useful independent verification is dimensional analysis, followed by one limiting case: for example, zero excitation, zero frequency, or no mechanical load. In magnetic systems, material saturation invalidates proportional field laws; in electric machines, parasitic resistance and friction introduce heat; and in measurements, uncertainty prevents an exact empirical conclusion.\n\n### Engineering workflow\n\n1. Draw and label the electrical or magnetic equivalent circuit and specify reference directions.\n2. Identify which parameters are given, measured, fitted or assumed ideal.\n3. Calculate one predicted output by hand with correct units and signs.\n4. Change one parameter by 10% and explain whether the response is linear or nonlinear.\n5. Compare conservation of energy and rate the result against thermal, electrical and mechanical operating limits.\n6. Document what this model omits before using it for an experiment or design.\n\n### Concept checks\n\n- Which assumption is most likely to fail near rated load, and what measurement would reveal it?\n- What physical quantity is stored rather than dissipated, and how would you verify the energy balance?\n- How does the predicted response change if the principal excitation is doubled, and why might a real device respond differently?\n\n\n\n### Comprehensive Theoretical Derivations & Analytical Modeling\n\nIn electrical machinery analysis, quantitative formulation begins from Maxwell's integral laws applied to ferromagnetic geometries. For 1.3 Nonlinear B-H curves and hysteresis, we define the magnetic circuit relationship:\n\n$$\\mathcal{F} = N I = \\oint \\vec{H} \\cdot d\\vec{l} = \\Phi \\left( \\mathcal{R}_{\\text{core}} + \\mathcal{R}_{\\text{gap}} \\right)$$\n\nwhere the reluctance $\\mathcal{R}$ is governed by path length $l$, cross-sectional area $A$, and effective permeability $\\mu = \\mu_0 \\mu_r$:\n\n$$\\mathcal{R} = \\frac{l}{\\mu A}$$\n\nWhen analyzing electromechanical energy conversion in Unit 1, the differential mechanical force developed along coordinate $x$ is derived rigorously from the magnetic coenergy $W_f'(i, x)$:\n\n$$F_e = \\left. \\frac{\\partial W_f'(i, x)}{\\partial x} \\right|_{i = \\text{const}} = \\frac{1}{2} i^2 \\frac{dL(x)}{dx}$$\n\nFor rotational devices, the developed electromagnetic torque becomes:\n\n$$T_e = \\left. \\frac{\\partial W_f'(i, \\theta)}{\\partial \\theta} \\right|_{i = \\text{const}} = \\frac{1}{2} i^2 \\frac{dL(\\theta)}{d\\theta}$$\n\nUnder steady-state machine operation, terminal voltage balances internal induced electromotive force $E_a$ and winding impedance drops:\n\n$$V_t = E_a \\pm I_a R_a$$\n\nwhere the sign reflects generator (+ for internal EMF) or motor (- for terminal supply) operating quadrants. Core loss segregation accounts for hysteresis loss $P_h = k_h f B_{\\max}^n$ and eddy current loss $P_e = k_e f^2 B_{\\max}^2$, providing complete physical fidelity.\n"
